---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Improve Your PTCL Speed — 10 Fixes That Work"
  description="Slow PTCL internet? Ten practical fixes ordered by impact — from testing over Ethernet and moving your router to checking your line and knowing when to call PTCL."
  canonical="/guides/improve-ptcl-speed"
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Improve PTCL Speed</p>
      <h1>How to Improve Your PTCL Speed</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Before you pay for a faster package, check whether you're actually getting the
        speed you already pay for. These fixes are ordered by impact — start at the top.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>1. Test with an Ethernet cable first</h2>
      <p>
        Plug a laptop directly into the router and <a href="/speed-test">run the test</a>.
        If the wired result is close to your package speed but Wi-Fi is slow, your line is
        fine — the problem is Wi-Fi, and fixes 2–5 will help. If the wired result is slow
        too, the problem is the line itself — skip to fix 6.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Move the router</h2>
      <p>
        Put the router in a central, open spot — ideally high up, away from walls, metal
        objects, mirrors and microwaves. A router hidden in a corner cabinet or behind the
        TV can lose half its signal before it leaves the room.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Restart the router properly</h2>
      <p>
        Unplug the router's power for 30 seconds, then plug it back in and wait two
        minutes. This clears its memory, re-establishes the DSL sync, and sometimes picks
        a less crowded Wi-Fi channel. Do this once a week if your connection degrades
        over time.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Use the 5 GHz band near the router, 2.4 GHz further away</h2>
      <p>
        Most PTCL routers broadcast two Wi-Fi networks. 5&nbsp;GHz is faster but has short
        range — use it in the same room as the router. 2.4&nbsp;GHz reaches further through
        walls but is slower and more crowded. If your router shows only one network name,
        both bands may be combined automatically.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Cut the devices you don't need</h2>
      <p>
        Every connected phone, smart TV and laptop shares your speed — and many keep
        downloading updates in the background. Pause cloud backups and game updates during
        work hours, and check your router's admin page for devices you don't recognise
        (someone may be using your Wi-Fi).
      </p>

      <h2>6. Check the phone line and splitter</h2>
      <p>
        On DSL, a damaged line is the most common cause of slow speed. Check for: loose
        connections at the wall socket, a missing or faulty splitter (the small box that
        separates phone and internet), and crackling on the phone line — noise on the line
        directly hurts DSL speed. Try connecting the router directly to the main socket,
        bypassing extensions.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Update your router's firmware</h2>
      <p>
        Old firmware can cause drops and poor Wi-Fi performance. Log in to your router
        (usually at 192.168.1.1 or 192.168.10.1) and check for a firmware update — or ask
        PTCL support to push one. If your router is more than five years old, a newer one
        is often the single biggest Wi-Fi upgrade.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Change your DNS</h2>
      <p>
        DNS doesn't change your speed test result, but it changes how fast websites
        <em>start</em> loading. Try Cloudflare (1.1.1.1) or Google (8.8.8.8) DNS in your
        router or device settings — many users find browsing feels noticeably snappier.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Test at different times</h2>
      <p>
        If your speed is fine at 10&nbsp;am but poor at 9&nbsp;pm, the problem is
        congestion in your area, not your home setup. Note the pattern — it's useful
        evidence when you complain, and it tells you whether an upgrade would even help.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Call PTCL with evidence</h2>
      <p>
        If wired tests are consistently well below your package speed, call PTCL helpline
        <strong>1218</strong> and report it. Have ready: your account number, your package
        speed, and dated speed test results (screenshot your results here, or use the
        "Copy result" button). Ask them to check your line's SNR and attenuation values —
        poor line quality is their side to fix, and they can send a technician.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>When nothing works:</strong> if your DSL line physically can't deliver
          more, ask whether Flash Fiber is available on your street. Fibre doesn't suffer
          from distance or line-noise problems at all — it's the real fix for a bad copper
          line.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Test your speed after each fix</h2>
        <p>Change one thing at a time and re-test — that's how you find what actually helped.</p>
        <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Run the Speed Test</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
